      en: "Mashhad is Iran's second-largest city and its spiritual capital, centered around the holy shrine of Imam Reza, the eighth Shia Imam. The Imam Reza Shrine complex is the largest mosque in the world by area and stands as a masterpiece of Islamic architecture, attracting over 30 million pilgrims and tourists annually. Located in northeastern Iran within the valley of the Kashaf River, Mashhad is flanked by the Hezar Masjed and Binalud mountain ranges, providing a cooler climate than the central deserts. The city has a storied history as a vital crossroads on the Silk Road and briefly served as the capital of the Afsharid Empire under Nader Shah. Beyond its religious preeminence, Mashhad is a powerhouse of the Iranian economy, world-famous for its production of saffron—often called 'Red Gold'—as well as exquisite carpets and gemstones. The surrounding relief offers diverse landscapes, from the terraced historical village of Kang to the lush summer retreats of Torghabeh and Shandiz. The region is also deeply connected to Persian identity as the burial place of Ferdowsi, the legendary poet of the Shahnameh, in nearby Tus. As a modern metropolis, Mashhad boasts an efficient metro system and an international airport to accommodate its vast seasonal population.",
